WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.017 --> 00:00:01.600
STEPHANIE WONG: Your
cloud application

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.110
has a lot of data moving
over many parts of the public

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:06.770
and the private internet.

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:08.740
But what if you
want a heavier hand

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.680
in defining exactly what
paths your data can take?

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.300
In this episode of
Cloud Networking,

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.980
we'll talk about how
to define traffic

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.220
routing between networks.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:16.720
Stay tuned.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:24.620
One common problem
with networking

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.560
is that you need the ability
to link networks together

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.290
without allowing the data
to be visible to every user

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:31.790
in every location.

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.550
For example, let's say
company A and B share

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.290
a common wall in a
building and each

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:38.450
have their own private
networks, but then wish

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:41.150
to connect them together without
giving up their current network

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.680
configuration.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.050
This would mean making both
networks use different IP

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.873
address ranges and
set up a router that

00:00:46.873 --> 00:00:48.290
connects to both
of these networks

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.030
to pass traffic between them.

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:51.590
Traditionally,
setting up a router

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:53.330
for an on-premise
network requires

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:55.340
a fair bit of heavy lifting.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.350
You physically assemble
these things together

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.540
to build interconnectivity
between users

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.400
and the applications they used.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.670
Implementing all of this
could take days or even weeks.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.460
And as the network grows,
the associated management

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.610
and cost to operate it also
grows disproportionately

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:12.110
larger for your company.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:15.620
But with Google Cloud Platform,
software-defined routing

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:19.580
means every VPC network uses
a scalable Distributed Virtual

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.170
Routing mechanism.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:25.200
GCP routes define the paths
network traffic takes from a VM

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.320
instance to other destinations.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.950
These destinations can be
inside your VPC network--

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:33.020
for example, in another VM--

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.325
or outside of it.

00:01:34.325 --> 00:01:37.010
Even though some routes
can be applied selectively,

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.750
the routing table
for a VPC network

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.760
is defined at the global
VPC network level.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.400
Let's take a closer look.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.290
Every route consists
of a destination

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:48.470
and a next hop, which are
IP addresses or ranges of IP

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:49.550
addresses.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.550
Traffic whose destination IP
is within the destination range

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:54.950
is sent to the next
hop for delivery.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:56.600
Each VM instance
has a controller

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.280
that's kept informed
of all its routes

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.440
from the network's
routing table.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.870
Each packet leaving a VM
is sent to the next hop

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:06.170
of an applicable route
based on a routing order.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.509
When you add or delete a
route, the set of changes

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:10.850
is propagated to
the VM controllers

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.260
using an eventually
consistent design.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.230
This means that
you have simplicity

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:18.980
and centralized control, a
high degree of automation,

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:20.900
built-in security
and encryption,

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.990
and high [? performance. ?]

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:26.230
Now, there are two types
of routes on a GCP VPC--

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:29.050
system-generated routes
or custom routes.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.780
System-generated routes give
you a default route and subnet

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:32.440
route.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.500
The default route defines a
path for traffic from your VPC

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.970
to Google services and
the public internet.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:40.390
The subnet route defines
a path for your traffic

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:43.030
into your VPC to each subnet.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.100
This is how VPC peering works.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.250
VPC peers always exchange
all subnet routes.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.040
Custom routes mean you create
static routing yourself

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.650
or dynamic routes using
Google Cloud Router.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:56.530
Let's walk through a
static route setup.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:02.170
Here, I have two VPCs set up
in US east and US central.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:04.750
I also set up a VPN
gateway on both sides

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:08.470
and created two IPSec
tunnels between them.

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:12.220
I have an Ohio server
in the US East VPC

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:16.420
and a warehouse server
in the US central VPC.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:19.660
But I still can't ping the
internal IP of the warehouse

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.910
server from the Ohio server.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:23.860
I need to create
two static routes

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.760
to enable traffic to be
forwarded into the tunnel.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.070
We have default routes
created for each network,

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:33.130
but we're going to create
custom static routes.

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:36.520
First, let's create a
route from east to central.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:38.640
Click CREATE ROUTE.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.280
Specify a name for the route.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.490
Select an existing network
where the route will apply.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.280
The Destination IP range is
a central subnet internal IP

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:48.540
range.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.372
I'll skip Priority
for now, but you

00:03:50.372 --> 00:03:52.080
would use this to
determine routing order

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.220
if routes have the
same destinations.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:57.070
Select a target
instance tag, or leave

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.950
it blank to apply the route to
all instances in the network.

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:01.930
Select a Next hop for the route.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:04.060
I'll select my VPN tunnel.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.210
And click Create.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.960
Now, I'll create another
route for traffic

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.980
from central to east.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.460
And voila, I can now ping the
internal IP of the warehouse

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.579
server from the Ohio server.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:19.170
Keep in mind that
with static routes,

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:21.870
you have to create or
maintain a routing table.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:23.850
And a topology change
on either network

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.730
requires you to manually
update static routes.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.310
Also, static routes can't
automatically reroute traffic

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:30.960
if a link fails.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.540
But static routing is
perfect for small networks

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:34.653
with stable topologies.

00:04:34.653 --> 00:04:36.570
There's so much more I
can say around routing,

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.880
but it's clear that
Google's virtual routing

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.370
is what connects all the
moving pieces between subnets

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.960
and a VPC, and even to your
local network on-premise.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:47.160
Stay tuned for more
networking end-to-end.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:48.870
And remember,
optimizing your network

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.120
means freeing up your bandwidth.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

